14. What We Learned
From this topic, we learned:

Inheritance allows one class to reuse another class's code.

The parent class contains common features.

The child class inherits those features and can add its own features.

We should use the IS-A test before using inheritance.

super().__init__() calls the parent class's constructor.

A child class can add its own attributes and methods.

A child class can override a parent method.

super() can be used inside an overridden method to reuse the parent's implementation.

Python looks for a method in the child class first and then in the parent class.

Inheritance reduces code duplication and improves code organization.

15. Easy Way to Remember
Parent = common features
Child = common features + special features
super() = use the parent's functionality
Override = replace/customize the parent's method
IS-A = inheritance relationship

Example:

Machine

   |

   ├── Lathe

   |

   ├── Press

   |

   ├── Drill

   |

   └── Grinder

The common machine features are written once in Machine, while each child class adds only what makes it different.


